From first circuit, we have
$i=\frac{\varepsilon}{\mathrm{R}+r}$
Value of $r$ is very less can be neglected as compared to R
Now,
$\begin{aligned}
i & =\frac{\varepsilon}{\mathrm{R}} \\
10^{-3} & =\frac{1}{\mathrm{R}} \\
\mathrm{R} & =10^3=1000 \Omega
\end{aligned}$
Now, from second circuit
$\begin{aligned}
i_1 & =\frac{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{eq}}}{\mathrm{R}+r_{e q}}=\frac{3}{1000} \\
i_1 & =3 \times 10^{-3} \\
& =3 \mathrm{~mA}
